#   estimates: "0.4d, 1.6d, 1.3d against 1d each -- 3.3d actual against 3d estimated, 1.1x. The first wave in four to run OVER, and the overrun is entirely fix cycles."
#   note: >-
#     THE WAVE'S THROUGH-LINE: three cards, and on every single one the thing that actually failed
#     was A GUARD CLAIMING COMPLETENESS IT DID NOT HAVE. CARD-245's write predicate was derived
#     from the class as its card demanded and was default-ALLOW underneath, so 8 of 9 real write
#     shapes were exempt; its replacement then treated a fresh container of references to live
#     rows as not-durable-state. CARD-224's docstring NAMED two property oracles as pinning the
#     boundary it cared about and both were structurally incapable of it -- one accepts a 2.0 mm
#     window against a 12 px threshold. CARD-246's second-renderer guard was literal, so a
#     renderer with different class names walked past it. AND THE ORCHESTRATOR PRODUCED ONE TOO:
#     the byte-identical merge check passed an unquoted multi-line variable as a pathspec, which
#     zsh does not word-split, so it matched nothing and reported success regardless of the tree.
#     Re-run per file with a control; both cards' conclusions held, but the method had been
#     worthless. The fix was the same one demanded of every card: pair the claim with a control
#     that can fail.
#   second_pattern: >-
#     FIVE suggested fixes in one wave could not do what their own finding asked. CARD-224: a
#     finer sweep resolution for a boundary needing an exact fraction unreachable by walking a
#     trim height; and a minimal clamp whose safe branch was provably unreachable above a 5.6 mm
#     band. CARD-246: an assertion about a POST that no longer happens once the withdrawal exists;
#     "disable Save", which drops keyboard focus mid-flow; and keying "was anything stored" on
#     status codes, which still claims on a 422 carrying an unrecognised reason. In four of the
#     five the fix agent caught it and said so with a demonstration. A review's FINDING and its
#     REMEDY deserve separate scrutiny -- the findings were right every time.
#   third_pattern: >-
#     Every important finding on CARD-245, across both of its review cycles, came from a SURVIVING
#     MUTANT rather than from reading the code. mutation_check: true earns its cost on this board.
#   gate_evidence_findings: >-
#     Two findings about the gate's own instruments, neither of which ever made a verdict wrong
#     (EXIT was the authority throughout) but both of which made evidence harder to read.
#     pyproject.toml already sets addopts = "-q", so a gate adding its own runs at -qq where
#     pytest prints NO summary count line at all -- very probably the real mechanism behind the
#     CARD-223 incident recorded as "a grep pattern that missed pytest's shape"; there was nothing
#     to match. And `-rs` reports skips but NOT failures in the summary block, so a red run printed
#     its count and traceback with no FAILED line; switched to `-rfs`. The gate's reporting flags
#     are load-bearing evidence and deserve the same scrutiny as the tests.
