# #   patching the reported instance, and declared a cost of its own fix.
# #
# # THE GATE WRAPPER'S exit 127 WAS MINE. I edited the running gate script, twice,
# #   while bash was executing it. Bash reads a script by byte offset, so changing
# #   its length shifted the offsets and bash resumed on a mangled line, whose
# #   failure the script captured instead of pytest's exit code. Pytest's own
# #   summary is the verdict and it is green; the trap still released the lock.
# #   A GATE REPORTING ONLY AN EXIT CODE WOULD HAVE READ RED ON A GREEN SUITE.
# #   Editing a file another process is reading is the plant-window hazard I had
# #   been briefing three rounds about the same day.
# #
# # AND I RETRACTED PART OF CARD-299'S MERGE RECORD. Its "0 rival pytest at both
# #   ends" was measuring nothing: the probe prints the executable name, which for
# #   this suite is the framework binary, so the grep cannot match a running suite.
# #   Five one-liner variants at one instant gave 0, 3, 2, 0, 2, because this host
# #   runs shells and search tools whose own command lines carry the pattern -- a
# #   command-line form over-counts as badly as an executable-name form under-
# #   counts. NO ONE-LINER GETS IT RIGHT, so the gate scripts now RECORD THE PID
# #   LIST for a human to read instead of reporting a count. The gate verdicts are
# #   untouched; only the host-quietness cell was blind.
# #   A mid-run warning of mine was wrong too: I read live database connections as
